  "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round1_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B021",
        "laureates": [
          "Antoine Georges",
          "Dieter Vollhardt",
          "Gabriel Kotliar"
        ],
        "citation": "for dynamical mean-field theory of strongly correlated electrons"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B002",
    "B015"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "DMFT is an unusually clean and durable theoretical achievement: it supplied a non-perturbative framework linking microscopic correlated-electron models to material properties, with extensions and use across many systems. The proposed trio represents the infinite-dimensional foundation and the formulation that made the method broadly effective. Its long record of reproducible application and experimental confrontation gives it the maturity expected of a prize.",
    "case_against": "The achievement is a framework rather than a sharply bounded discrete discovery, and its development and practical extensions involve a wider community. The final historical review must establish whether these three names capture the essential conceptual steps without understating other contributors.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
        "point": "Eriksson argues that DMFT provides a quantitatively useful bridge between microscopic models and measured correlated-material properties, while cautioning that it is a method with a substantial wider developer community.",
        "response": "I agree with both parts of this assessment. The methodological character is a real attribution constraint, but it is also the scientific strength of the case: DMFT isolated a general, analysable mechanism for treating local correlations and thereby reorganized a large field. The three proposed names should be retained only if archival review confirms their distinct foundational roles."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll emphasizes that optical lattice clocks have clear complementary attribution to Katori and Ye, exceptional reproducibility, and demonstrated metrological and fundamental-physics value.",
        "response": "This is the strongest alternative. The clock case has a particularly elegant pairing of concept and realization, but its final citation must separate the lattice-clock breakthrough from the wider optical-clock enterprise. I still prefer DMFT because its central theoretical construction has transformed the understanding and calculation of correlated matter over a longer and broader range of physical systems."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "olsson-eva",
        "point": "Olsson stresses that aberration-corrected TEM is a mature, transformative atomic-scale probe, while noting that the division between conceptual and engineering priority needs archival verification.",
        "response": "I find the instrumental transformation compelling and regard it as a close alternative. Yet the stated uncertainty is consequential: the achievement joins tightly coupled electron-optical, engineering, and deployment advances. DMFT presently offers the more coherent three-person conceptual attribution, subject to the same careful historical review."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether the founding DMFT contribution can be delimited sufficiently clearly from its numerous later extensions and implementations.",
      "Whether archival priority evidence supports the proposed division of credit among Vollhardt, Georges, and Kotliar."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No candidate-specific conflict is apparent from the supplied profile. I share a Gothenburg physics environment with Göran Johansson and Eva Olsson, but no specific collaboration or connection to the proposed laureates is indicated."
  }
}
